ell_type":"Astrocytes","gene_id":"ENSG00000241468","module":"turquoise","kme":0.169486,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.500479,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"CUX2+","gene_id":"ENSG00000241468","module":"turquoise","kme":0.364538,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.789273,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"CUX2-","gene_id":"ENSG00000241468","module":"turquoise","kme":0.36519,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.787198,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"Inhibitory","gene_id":"ENSG00000241468","module":"turquoise","kme":0.26059,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.795497,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"Microglia","gene_id":"ENSG00000241468","module":"turquoise","kme":0.155327,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.396297,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"Oligodendroglia","gene_id":"ENSG00000241468","module":"brown","kme":0.191231,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.137697,"module_significant":false},{"cell_type":"Vascular.niche","gene_id":"ENSG00000241468","module":"pink","kme":0.281131,"hub":false,"module_direction":"higher in AD","module_padj":0.104801,"module_significant":false}],"degas_bulk_de":false,"drug_target":false}},"description":[{"gene_id":"ENSG00000241468","entrez_id":["9551"],"uniprot":["P56134"],"ncbi_summary":"Mitochondrial ATP synthase catalyzes ATP synthesis, utilizing an electrochemical gradient of protons across the inner membrane during oxidative phosphorylation. It is composed of two linked multi-subunit complexes: the soluble catalytic core, F1, and the membrane-spanning component, Fo, which comprises the proton channel. The catalytic portion of mitochondrial ATP synthase consists of five different subunits (alpha, beta, gamma, delta, and epsilon) assembled with a stoichiometry of 3 alpha, 3 beta, and single representatives of the gamma, delta, and epsilon subunits. The proton channel likely has nine subunits (a, b, c, d, e, f, g, F6 and 8). This gene encodes the f subunit of the Fo complex. Alternatively spliced transcript variants encoding different isoforms have been identified for this gene. This gene has multiple pseudogenes. Naturally occurring read-through transcription also exists between this gene and the downstream pentatricopeptide repeat domain 1 (PTCD1) gene. [provided by RefSeq, Nov 2010].","ncbi_source":"NCBI Gene summary (RefSeq), via MyGene.info","uniprot_function":[{"accession":"P56134","text":"Subunit f, of the mitochondrial membrane ATP synthase complex (F(1)F(0) ATP synthase or Complex V) that produces ATP from ADP in the presence of a proton gradient across the membrane which is generated by electron transport complexes of the respiratory chain (PubMed:37244256). ATP synthase complex consist of a soluble F(1) head domain - the catalytic core - and a membrane F(1) domain - the membrane proton channel (PubMed:37244256). These two domains are linked by a central stalk rotating inside the F(1) region and a stationary peripheral stalk (PubMed:37244256). During catalysis, ATP synthesis in the catalytic domain of F(1) is coupled via a rotary mechanism of the central stalk subunits to proton translocation (Probable). In vivo, can only synthesize ATP although its ATP hydrolase activity can be activated artificially in vitro (By similarity). Part of the complex F(0) domain (PubMed:37244256)."}],"uniprot_source":"UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot Function, release 2026_03","retrieved":"2026-10-21"}],"structures":[],"alphafold_accessions":[]}